course: biology
lesson: 356
title: Nails, sweat and smells: smelly feet, armpits and bad breath
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Nails are plates of keratin, the same tough stuff as hair, growing out from a root under the skin to guard your fingertips and help you pick up small things.
explain: Most body smells are not made by you at all but by bacteria. Fresh sweat hardly smells; it is the tiny bacteria that eat it that make the pong.
explain: Pull off a pair of trainers after football and the cheesy smell is a real cousin of cheese: some of the same bacteria help ripen smelly cheeses.
explain: The cure for most smells is the same: wash the bacteria off, dry the skin, air the shoes, brush teeth and tongue, and drink water.
fact: Fingernails grow about three millimetres a month and toenails more slowly; a whole fingernail takes about six months to grow out and a toenail a year or more.
fact: Nails often grow a little faster in summer and on the hand you use most.
fact: The white half-moon at the base of a nail, the lunula, is part of the nail root and is often biggest on the thumbs.
fact: People have twenty nails, ten on the fingers and ten on the toes; biting nails carries germs from the hands to the mouth.
fact: A pair of feet has about 250,000 sweat glands; bacteria eat the sweat and make cheesy smells, and warm, dark shoes help them grow.
fact: Around puberty special sweat glands in the armpits switch on; their sweat is not smelly until bacteria eat it, which is why a wash helps and why older children use deodorant.
fact: Some people, more often in East Asia, have a family trait, passed down from their parents, that gives very little armpit smell and dry, flaky earwax.
fact: At night you make less saliva, so bacteria on the teeth and tongue grow and make smelly gases, which is morning breath; brushing the tongue and drinking water help.
fact: Garlic substances pass into the blood and come out through the lungs and even the sweat, so brushing teeth cannot fully stop garlic breath.
fact: Most fart gas has no smell; tiny amounts of sulphur gases make the smell, and gut bacteria make extra gas from food we cannot digest, like beans.
fact: People fart roughly 10 to 20 times a day, which is normal.
fact: Dogs can smell sweat and skin smells we cannot notice, each person has their own smell, and trained dogs have been reported in research to sniff out some illnesses.
fact: Cutting toenails straight across helps stop ingrown toenails, where the edge grows into the skin and can get sore and infected.
exercise: About how fast do fingernails grow? | About three millimetres a month | About three centimetres a day | About one millimetre a year | About ten centimetres a month
exercise: Which grow faster, fingernails or toenails? | Fingernails | Toenails | They grow at exactly the same speed | Neither grows after childhood
exercise: What is the white half-moon at the base of a nail? | Part of the nail root, called the lunula | A sign of too little calcium | A patch of dead germs | A small bruise under the nail
exercise: How many nails does a person usually have? | 20 | 10 | 24 | 18
exercise: Why can nail biting make you ill? | It carries germs from hands to mouth | Nails are poisonous if you swallow little bits of them | It makes the teeth fall out | It stops the nails protecting you
exercise: What actually makes sweaty feet smell? | Bacteria eating the sweat | The sweat itself as it comes out | Socks rotting from the heat | Dead toenails
exercise: Why do trainers often smell more than sandals? | Warm, dark, damp shoes help bacteria grow | Trainers are made from a smelly rubber that rots in the heat | Sandals block sweat glands | Feet sweat only in trainers
exercise: When do the armpit sweat glands that cause body smell switch on? | Around puberty | At birth | When you are very old | Only in winter
exercise: Why does a shower help with armpit smell? | It washes away the sweat and the bacteria that eat it | It closes all the sweat glands for the day | It kills every germ for a week | Hot water makes the sweat itself start to smell nice and fresh
exercise: Why is breath often smelly in the morning? | Less saliva at night lets bacteria grow | Your teeth rot a little bit more every single night | The stomach empties into the mouth | Air in the bedroom is stale
exercise: Why does garlic breath last even after brushing? | Garlic substances come out through the lungs from the blood | Garlic sticks to the teeth like glue and will not brush off | Toothpaste reacts with garlic | Garlic grows in the stomach
exercise: What makes the smell in a fart? | Tiny amounts of sulphur gases | All of the gas smells the same | Bits of undigested beans | Air that was swallowed
exercise: Why do beans give you more wind? | Gut bacteria make gas from parts we cannot digest | Beans are full of tiny air bubbles that get let out as you chew | Beans swell up and burst | Beans are digested in the lungs
exercise: Why can a dog recognise you by smell? | Each person has their own smell, and dogs smell far better than we do | Dogs read your name tag | Everyone smells the same to a dog | Dogs only recognise faces
exercise: How should toenails be cut to avoid ingrown nails? | Straight across | In a deep curve at the corners | As short as possible | Into a sharp point
test: Count them: a family of four people. How many nails do they have in all? | 80 | 40 | 20 | 100
test: Count them: a boy paints a dot at the base of his thumbnail. Nails grow about 3 mm a month. How far up has the dot moved after 2 months? | About 6 mm | About 3 mm | About 2 cm | About 1 mm
test: «me feet stink, means I'm dirty inside» What is right? | No, bacteria on the skin eat sweat and make the smell | Yes, the smell comes from deep inside your body and leaks out | Yes, smelly feet mean you are ill | No, the smell comes from your socks only
test: «brushed me teeth twice after the garlic bread, why do I still stink» What is the truth? | Garlic comes out through the lungs from the blood, so brushing cannot stop it | He did not brush long enough | Garlic is stuck behind the teeth | Toothpaste makes garlic smell stronger
test: In a cartoon a skunk's smell is shown as a green cloud, and Tom's sweaty socks make flowers wilt and flies drop dead. What is the truth about sweaty socks? | The smell is from bacteria and is unpleasant, but it does not kill flowers or flies | Sock smell really is green and poisonous | Sweaty socks can kill small animals | The smell comes from the socks themselves, not from bacteria
test: «my little brother farts like 15 times a day, is he ill» What is right? | About 10 to 20 times a day is normal | Yes, more than once a day means illness | Yes, only grown-ups fart | No, children never fart that much
test: What would most likely happen if you sipped water and brushed your tongue every morning? | Morning breath would be less smelly | Your teeth would turn white overnight | You would stop making saliva | Your tongue would grow back thicker
test: «older cousin pongs and he showers every day, why» Which is the most likely reason? | New armpit glands since puberty make sweat that bacteria love | He must be ill with something serious that makes his whole body smell | His shower water is dirty | He sweats only on his feet
test: Why do toenails take longer to grow out than fingernails? | Toenails grow more slowly | Toenails are much longer | Toenails stop growing in winter | Toenails grow backwards
test: Mum cuts a toenail into a deep curve at the corner, and a week later the toe is red and sore. What has probably happened? | The nail edge has grown into the skin | The toe is broken | The nail has fallen off | The toe has a bruise from the scissors
test: «dogs can smell when you're ill, it's all made up» What is fairest to say? | Trained dogs have been reported in research to sniff out some illnesses | It is fully proven that dogs can sniff out every illness | Dogs cannot smell people at all | Dogs smell only food, never people
exam: Identify the principal cause of the odour associated with perspiring feet. | The action of bacteria which consume the perspiration | The perspiration as it is first secreted | The decomposition of the stockings | The toenails as they age
exam: State the aggregate number of nails ordinarily possessed by one person. | Twenty | Ten | Twenty-four | Eighteen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the persistence of odour subsequent to the consumption of garlic? | Substances derived from garlic pass into the blood and are exhaled by the lungs | The odour is retained solely upon the surface of the teeth | The odour is generated by the toothpaste | The garlic continues to grow within the stomach
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the manner in which toenails are properly to be cut. | They are to be cut straight across | They are to be cut in a deep curve at the corners | They are to be cut to a sharp point | They are to be cut as short as practicable
